No. Regional and global secrets are separate resources behind different endpoints, even when they share the same ID.

- `gcloud secrets list` without the endpoint override queries `secretmanager.googleapis.com`, which returns only global secrets.
- Regional secrets exist only on `secretmanager.me-central2.rep.googleapis.com` (your `rg` function), so the loop never touches them.

**Safe check before running (info only; both lists are separate):**

```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
echo "GLOBAL:";   gcloud secrets list --project=$P --filter="name~CN_STAGING_" --format="value(name)" | head -3
echo "REGIONAL:"; CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER=https://secretmanager.me-central2.rep.googleapis.com/ gcloud secrets list --location=me-central2 --project=$P --format="value(name)" | head -3
```

Global names look like `projects/<num>/secrets/...`; regional ones look like `projects/<num>/locations/me-central2/secrets/...`.

**Caveat:** make sure your shell does not have `CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER` exported permanently (`echo $CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER` should print nothing). If it is exported, the loop would hit the regional endpoint instead. Since you use `rg` as a function, it should be empty.

**After running, confirm regional is intact:**

```bash
CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER=https://secretmanager.me-central2.rep.googleapis.com/ gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=CN_STAGING_DB_HOST --location=me-central2 --project=$P >/dev/null && echo "regional OK"
```
